  "review": {
    "label": "curated_editorial",
    "look_ahead": "The 2020-21 season would end the McInnes era and begin the most volatile managerial sequence of the period.\n",
    "read": "This is the control case: a stable, senior squad under a long-serving manager, finishing where the previous seasons had finished. It is not yet evidence for or against the trading model. Its value is as a comparison — the later seasons show what changed when the squad, the manager and the recruitment pattern changed.\n",
    "trade_off": "Continuity protected the league floor but did not change the European ceiling. The first Cormack season is the baseline against which the later turnover is measured.\n",
    "what_did_not": "The European campaign ended at the third qualifying round for the sixth year in a row, and the Scottish Cup semi-final was lost without a goal. The league position was decided by average points per game, not a full season.\n",
    "what_worked": "The senior squad held a top-four position through a curtailed league and reached the Scottish Cup semi-final and League Cup quarter-final. The transition from Milne to Cormack did not produce a mid-season collapse.\n"
  },
